From cdfac1aa8867120499b4d5dc47257d6500274026
Subject: transparent focus target for depth of field
Origin: https://github.com/robtfm/bevy/commit/cdfac1aa8867120499b4d5dc47257d6500274026
Date: 2026-07-16T10:31:39+01:00

Alpha-blended materials previously wrote depth in the main pass
(4dfb0a192) so dof wouldn't blur opaque-alpha pixels against the
background, but the depth write also depth-tested transparent
fragments against each other: the back-to-front sort is per-object
while the test is per-pixel, so a transparent fragment behind an
already-drawn transparent pixel was dropped entirely.

Revert blend pipelines to depth-write-off and instead give the main
transparent pass an extra Rg16Float target when the view has depth of
field. Fragments alpha-blend (view_depth, 1, 0, alpha) into it, so it
accumulates alpha-weighted view depth (r) and total coverage (g); the
dof shader computes focus depth as r + (1 - g) * opaque_depth, with
max_depth applied to the opaque term before the blend. Transparent
surfaces attract focus in proportion to their opacity, and
transparent-vs-transparent occlusion can no longer reject fragments.

FragmentOutput gains the output behind TRANSPARENT_FOCUS_OUTPUT; wgsl
zero-init plus over-blending makes shaders that don't set it exact
no-ops, so existing custom material shaders keep working unmodified.
Pipelines that render in the transparent pass but never write focus
(gizmos) declare the target with an empty write mask. R32Float with
max-blending would be simpler but 32-bit float blending needs
FLOAT32_BLENDABLE, which wgpu 24 doesn't expose; f16 linear view-z
avoids the subnormal flush that f16 reverse-z hits at distance.
